<h2>How This 1 Rep Max Calculator Works</h2>
<p>Your one-rep max (1RM) is the most weight you can lift for a single rep with good form. Testing it directly is tiring and carries more injury risk, so most coaches estimate it from a heavy set of 2 to 10 reps instead.</p>
<p>This calculator averages three of the most widely used formulas, <strong>Epley</strong>, <strong>Brzycki</strong> and <strong>Lombardi</strong>, and shows each one so you can see how close they agree. Estimates are most accurate from sets of <strong>10 reps or fewer</strong>, taken close to failure. Past 10 reps the formulas drift apart, so the calculator flags those results as rough.</p>
<h2>1RM Formulas</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>Equation</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Epley</td><td>weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</td><td>Most common; reads slightly high at low reps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brzycki</td><td>weight × 36 ÷ (37 − reps)</td><td>Accurate up to about 10 reps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lombardi</td><td>weight × reps<sup>0.10</sup></td><td>Conservative at higher reps</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h2>Strength Standards for Athletes</h2>
<p>These levels compare your estimated 1RM with your bodyweight. They are approximate targets for athletes, not powerlifting competition standards. The squat levels match the ones used on our <a href="/squat-strength-calculator">squat strength calculator</a>.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Lift (Male)</th><th>HS Athlete</th><th>College Athlete</th><th>Strong</th><th>Elite</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
